1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,590
Sweetheart, every Wednesday of my childhood was

2
00:00:03,590 --> 00:00:04,736
meatloaf night.

3
00:00:04,916 --> 00:00:07,736
My father came home from the Westinghouse

4
00:00:07,736 --> 00:00:09,525
plant at a quarter to six,

5
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:11,588
and he could smell it from the

6
00:00:11,588 --> 00:00:12,207
sidewalk.

7
00:00:12,387 --> 00:00:14,521
He'd open the door and say,

8
00:00:14,521 --> 00:00:17,050
Dorothy, is that what I think it

9
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:17,287
is?

10
00:00:17,467 --> 00:00:19,211
Every single Wednesday,

11
00:00:19,211 --> 00:00:20,880
like he was surprised.

12
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:22,962
Thirty years of Wednesdays,

13
00:00:22,962 --> 00:00:25,075
and he never once got tired of

14
00:00:25,075 --> 00:00:25,286
it.

15
00:00:25,637 --> 00:00:29,187
And today, when my grandchildren make meatloaf,

16
00:00:29,187 --> 00:00:31,151
it comes out like a brick.

17
00:00:31,331 --> 00:00:34,594
Dry, crumbly, gray inside.

18
00:00:34,774 --> 00:00:37,557
So tonight I'm giving you my mother's

19
00:00:37,557 --> 00:00:40,717
meatloaf, every ingredient and every step,

20
00:00:40,717 --> 00:00:43,726
and the four mistakes that make meatloaf

21
00:00:43,726 --> 00:00:44,027
dry.

22
00:00:44,207 --> 00:00:46,889
It costs less than ten dollars,

23
00:00:46,889 --> 00:00:48,014
it feeds six,

24
00:00:48,014 --> 00:00:51,475
and the leftovers make the best sandwich

25
00:00:51,475 --> 00:00:52,427
in America.

26
00:00:52,777 --> 00:00:54,552
In nineteen fifty-two,

27
00:00:54,552 --> 00:00:57,456
two pounds of ground beef cost about

28
00:00:57,456 --> 00:00:59,474
a dollar ten at the A and

29
00:00:59,474 --> 00:01:00,845
P on Park Avenue.

30
00:01:01,025 --> 00:01:03,386
My mother bought it on Tuesday,

31
00:01:03,386 --> 00:01:05,290
after my father's payday,

32
00:01:05,290 --> 00:01:08,032
and every penny of that meatloaf was

33
00:01:08,032 --> 00:01:08,642
counted.

34
00:01:08,822 --> 00:01:11,462
That's why nothing went to waste,

35
00:01:11,462 --> 00:01:13,063
not the stale bread,

36
00:01:13,063 --> 00:01:14,503
not the drippings,

37
00:01:14,503 --> 00:01:16,983
not a single slice on Thursday.

38
00:01:17,163 --> 00:01:19,848
Meatloaf wasn't a fancy dinner,

39
00:01:19,848 --> 00:01:20,368
honey.

40
00:01:20,548 --> 00:01:22,429
It was a smart one.

41
00:01:22,779 --> 00:01:24,449
Here's what you need,

42
00:01:24,449 --> 00:01:24,926
honey.

43
00:01:25,106 --> 00:01:27,112
Two pounds of ground beef,

44
00:01:27,112 --> 00:01:28,810
and not the lean kind.

45
00:01:28,990 --> 00:01:30,621
Eighty percent lean,

46
00:01:30,621 --> 00:01:32,171
twenty percent fat.

47
00:01:32,351 --> 00:01:34,638
The fat is what keeps it juicy.

48
00:01:34,818 --> 00:01:36,165
One cup of milk.

49
00:01:36,345 --> 00:01:39,262
One cup of crushed saltine crackers,

50
00:01:39,262 --> 00:01:41,451
about twenty-four crackers,

51
00:01:41,451 --> 00:01:44,287
or three slices of white bread torn

52
00:01:44,287 --> 00:01:44,530
up.

53
00:01:44,710 --> 00:01:47,613
One onion, chopped very fine.

54
00:01:47,793 --> 00:01:48,884
Two eggs.

55
00:01:49,064 --> 00:01:50,776
One teaspoon of salt,

56
00:01:50,776 --> 00:01:52,896
half a teaspoon of pepper,

57
00:01:52,896 --> 00:01:56,239
and a tablespoon of Worcestershire sauce.

58
00:01:56,589 --> 00:01:58,600
And for the glaze on top,

59
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:01,093
the part everybody fights over.

60
00:02:01,273 --> 00:02:02,960
Half a cup of ketchup,

61
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:05,337
two tablespoons of brown sugar,

62
00:02:05,337 --> 00:02:08,634
and one tablespoon of plain yellow mustard.

63
00:02:08,814 --> 00:02:11,018
Stir it in a little bowl and

64
00:02:11,018 --> 00:02:12,041
set it aside.

65
00:02:12,221 --> 00:02:14,634
My mother mixed it in a teacup,

66
00:02:14,634 --> 00:02:17,436
always the same chipped blue teacup,

67
00:02:17,436 --> 00:02:19,849
and I still use that cup today.

68
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,383
One more thing about the beef,

69
00:02:22,383 --> 00:02:23,183
sweetheart.

70
00:02:23,363 --> 00:02:25,351
Take it out of the icebox about

71
00:02:25,351 --> 00:02:27,404
twenty minutes before you start.

72
00:02:27,584 --> 00:02:30,625
Ice-cold meat straight from the package is

73
00:02:30,625 --> 00:02:32,001
hard to mix gently,

74
00:02:32,001 --> 00:02:34,173
so people squeeze it too hard,

75
00:02:34,173 --> 00:02:36,707
and that's the beginning of a tough

76
00:02:36,707 --> 00:02:37,359
meatloaf.

77
00:02:37,539 --> 00:02:39,211
Cool, not cold.

78
00:02:39,391 --> 00:02:41,971
And wash your hands with cold water

79
00:02:41,971 --> 00:02:43,076
before you mix,

80
00:02:43,076 --> 00:02:45,361
so the fat doesn't melt on your

81
00:02:45,361 --> 00:02:45,951
fingers.

82
00:02:46,301 --> 00:02:48,123
Now here's the secret,

83
00:02:48,123 --> 00:02:51,850
sweetheart, the one nobody tells you anymore.

84
00:02:52,030 --> 00:02:53,412
Before anything else,

85
00:02:53,412 --> 00:02:55,780
put the crushed crackers in your big

86
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:57,688
bowl and pour the cup of milk

87
00:02:57,688 --> 00:02:58,346
over them.

88
00:02:58,526 --> 00:03:00,734
Stir it and let it sit for

89
00:03:00,734 --> 00:03:04,132
five minutes, until it's soft and mushy,

90
00:03:04,132 --> 00:03:05,237
like oatmeal.

91
00:03:05,417 --> 00:03:08,103
That mush holds the juice inside the

92
00:03:08,103 --> 00:03:09,596
meat while it bakes.

93
00:03:09,776 --> 00:03:12,231
Without it, the juice runs out into

94
00:03:12,231 --> 00:03:14,757
the pan and your meatloaf dries out.

95
00:03:15,107 --> 00:03:17,939
Grandma Pearl called it the soak.

96
00:03:18,119 --> 00:03:21,142
During the Depression she used stale bread,

97
00:03:21,142 --> 00:03:23,814
because nobody threw away stale bread,

98
00:03:23,814 --> 00:03:25,923
and she'd say it made the meat

99
00:03:25,923 --> 00:03:26,978
go further too.

100
00:03:27,158 --> 00:03:29,352
She was right on both counts.

101
00:03:29,532 --> 00:03:31,723
Two pounds of beef with the soak

102
00:03:31,723 --> 00:03:32,887
feeds six people.

103
00:03:33,067 --> 00:03:35,374
Without it, it feeds four,

104
00:03:35,374 --> 00:03:36,527
and it's dry.

105
00:03:36,878 --> 00:03:38,552
And if you want to know if

106
00:03:38,552 --> 00:03:40,935
it's seasoned right before it goes in

107
00:03:40,935 --> 00:03:42,996
the oven, do what my mother did.

108
00:03:43,176 --> 00:03:45,658
Pinch off a little piece the size

109
00:03:45,658 --> 00:03:46,636
of a quarter,

110
00:03:46,636 --> 00:03:48,667
flatten it, and fry it in a

111
00:03:48,667 --> 00:03:50,246
skillet for a minute.

112
00:03:50,426 --> 00:03:51,297
Taste it.

113
00:03:51,477 --> 00:03:54,310
Too bland, add a little more salt.

114
00:03:54,490 --> 00:03:57,242
Too plain, a little more Worcestershire.

115
00:03:57,422 --> 00:03:59,271
It's the only way to know,

116
00:03:59,271 --> 00:04:01,759
because you can't fix the salt once

117
00:04:01,759 --> 00:04:02,542
it's baked.

118
00:04:02,892 --> 00:04:03,878
Step one.

119
00:04:04,058 --> 00:04:06,389
Heat your oven to three hundred and

120
00:04:06,389 --> 00:04:07,321
fifty degrees.

121
00:04:07,501 --> 00:04:10,036
Chop the onion as fine as you

122
00:04:10,036 --> 00:04:11,785
can, almost to mush.

123
00:04:11,965 --> 00:04:14,783
Big pieces of onion make the loaf

124
00:04:14,783 --> 00:04:17,259
fall apart when you slice it.

125
00:04:17,439 --> 00:04:20,216
If your grandchildren don't like onion,

126
00:04:20,216 --> 00:04:22,209
grate it on the big holes of

127
00:04:22,209 --> 00:04:23,349
a cheese grater.

128
00:04:23,529 --> 00:04:26,033
It melts right in and they'll never

129
00:04:26,033 --> 00:04:26,605
find it.

130
00:04:26,956 --> 00:04:28,024
Step two.

131
00:04:28,204 --> 00:04:31,131
Into the bowl with the soaked crackers,

132
00:04:31,131 --> 00:04:32,106
add the eggs,

133
00:04:32,106 --> 00:04:33,608
the onion, the salt,

134
00:04:33,608 --> 00:04:36,159
the pepper and the Worcestershire,

135
00:04:36,159 --> 00:04:37,735
and stir it together.

136
00:04:37,915 --> 00:04:40,249
Then add the ground beef on top.

137
00:04:40,429 --> 00:04:41,938
And now, honey,

138
00:04:41,938 --> 00:04:43,448
use your hands.

139
00:04:43,628 --> 00:04:44,648
Mix it gently,

140
00:04:44,648 --> 00:04:45,959
with your fingers,

141
00:04:45,959 --> 00:04:48,655
just until everything comes together.

142
00:04:48,835 --> 00:04:50,785
Not one second longer.

143
00:04:51,136 --> 00:04:52,273
Step three.

144
00:04:52,453 --> 00:04:54,902
Shape it on a baking sheet lined

145
00:04:54,902 --> 00:04:57,121
with foil, not in a loaf pan.

146
00:04:57,301 --> 00:04:59,999
Make a loaf about nine inches long

147
00:04:59,999 --> 00:05:01,665
and five inches wide,

148
00:05:01,665 --> 00:05:03,094
and pat it smooth.

149
00:05:03,274 --> 00:05:05,024
In a loaf pan it sits in

150
00:05:05,024 --> 00:05:06,919
its own grease and steams.

151
00:05:07,099 --> 00:05:08,032
On a sheet,

152
00:05:08,032 --> 00:05:09,473
the fat runs off,

153
00:05:09,473 --> 00:05:12,439
the sides get a little brown crust,

154
00:05:12,439 --> 00:05:15,575
and the glaze caramelizes all around.

155
00:05:15,925 --> 00:05:16,784
Step four.

156
00:05:16,964 --> 00:05:19,286
Bake it for forty-five minutes.

157
00:05:19,466 --> 00:05:20,641
Then take it out,

158
00:05:20,641 --> 00:05:22,923
spread the glaze all over the top

159
00:05:22,923 --> 00:05:24,791
and the sides with a spoon,

160
00:05:24,791 --> 00:05:26,727
and put it back in for about

161
00:05:26,727 --> 00:05:28,179
fifteen more minutes,

162
00:05:28,179 --> 00:05:30,600
until the glaze is dark and sticky.

163
00:05:30,780 --> 00:05:32,878
If you have a meat thermometer,

164
00:05:32,878 --> 00:05:35,451
the middle should read one hundred and

165
00:05:35,451 --> 00:05:36,399
sixty degrees.

166
00:05:36,579 --> 00:05:39,342
That's done, and not a minute more.

167
00:05:39,692 --> 00:05:42,815
Step five, and don't skip this one.

168
00:05:42,995 --> 00:05:44,938
Let it rest on the counter for

169
00:05:44,938 --> 00:05:47,012
ten minutes before you slice it.

170
00:05:47,192 --> 00:05:49,343
If you cut it straight from the

171
00:05:49,343 --> 00:05:51,841
oven, it crumbles and the juice runs

172
00:05:51,841 --> 00:05:53,159
all over the board.

173
00:05:53,339 --> 00:05:56,396
Ten minutes, and it slices clean like

174
00:05:56,396 --> 00:05:56,892
bread.

175
00:05:57,072 --> 00:05:59,956
That's when my mother mashed the potatoes,

176
00:05:59,956 --> 00:06:02,359
so everything was ready at the same

177
00:06:02,359 --> 00:06:02,703
time.

178
00:06:03,053 --> 00:06:04,908
And here's a little trick for a

179
00:06:04,908 --> 00:06:07,302
meatloaf that looks pretty on the table.

180
00:06:07,482 --> 00:06:09,266
Before it goes in the oven,

181
00:06:09,266 --> 00:06:11,645
press a long shallow groove down the

182
00:06:11,645 --> 00:06:13,693
middle of the top with the side

183
00:06:13,693 --> 00:06:14,552
of your hand.

184
00:06:14,732 --> 00:06:17,110
The glaze sits in that groove like

185
00:06:17,110 --> 00:06:19,488
a little river and doesn't all run

186
00:06:19,488 --> 00:06:20,468
off the sides.

187
00:06:20,648 --> 00:06:22,557
My mother did it with the edge

188
00:06:22,557 --> 00:06:23,257
of a spoon,

189
00:06:23,257 --> 00:06:25,740
and her meatloaf always looked like the

190
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:27,904
picture in the Betty Crocker book.

191
00:06:28,254 --> 00:06:29,987
Now the four mistakes,

192
00:06:29,987 --> 00:06:30,854
sweetheart.

193
00:06:31,034 --> 00:06:32,369
Mistake one.

194
00:06:32,549 --> 00:06:34,151
Using lean beef.

195
00:06:34,331 --> 00:06:36,918
Ninety percent lean is fine for a

196
00:06:36,918 --> 00:06:38,015
lot of things,

197
00:06:38,015 --> 00:06:39,347
but not meatloaf.

198
00:06:39,527 --> 00:06:41,857
It comes out dry no matter what

199
00:06:41,857 --> 00:06:42,383
you do.

200
00:06:42,563 --> 00:06:44,235
If you want it lighter,

201
00:06:44,235 --> 00:06:46,778
mix half beef and half ground pork,

202
00:06:46,778 --> 00:06:49,322
the way the Hungarian ladies at our

203
00:06:49,322 --> 00:06:50,121
church did.

204
00:06:50,301 --> 00:06:52,321
Never all lean beef.

205
00:06:52,672 --> 00:06:54,041
Mistake two.

206
00:06:54,221 --> 00:06:55,754
Mixing it too much.

207
00:06:55,934 --> 00:06:58,434
When you squeeze and knead it like

208
00:06:58,434 --> 00:07:01,081
bread dough, the meat gets tight and

209
00:07:01,081 --> 00:07:02,993
tough, like a hockey puck.

210
00:07:03,173 --> 00:07:05,886
Mix gently, only until you can't see

211
00:07:05,886 --> 00:07:07,016
streaks of egg.

212
00:07:07,196 --> 00:07:09,272
My mother said treat it like you're

213
00:07:09,272 --> 00:07:10,340
tucking a baby in,

214
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:12,594
not like you're punching your brother.

215
00:07:12,945 --> 00:07:14,384
Mistake three.

216
00:07:14,564 --> 00:07:15,992
Skipping the soak.

217
00:07:16,172 --> 00:07:18,468
Dry crumbs straight from the box pull

218
00:07:18,468 --> 00:07:20,516
the juice out of the meat instead

219
00:07:20,516 --> 00:07:21,571
of holding it in.

220
00:07:21,751 --> 00:07:24,259
Always soak them in milk first.

221
00:07:24,439 --> 00:07:26,076
And mistake four.

222
00:07:26,256 --> 00:07:27,475
Overbaking it.

223
00:07:27,655 --> 00:07:29,604
People leave it in for an hour

224
00:07:29,604 --> 00:07:31,033
and a half to be safe,

225
00:07:31,033 --> 00:07:33,177
and they bake every drop of juice

226
00:07:33,177 --> 00:07:33,436
out.

227
00:07:33,616 --> 00:07:35,221
Use a thermometer,

228
00:07:35,221 --> 00:07:37,449
take it out at one sixty,

229
00:07:37,449 --> 00:07:38,876
and let it rest.

230
00:07:39,226 --> 00:07:40,232
In our house,

231
00:07:40,232 --> 00:07:43,717
meatloaf always came with mashed potatoes and

232
00:07:43,717 --> 00:07:46,737
green beans, and nothing else would do.

233
00:07:46,917 --> 00:07:49,889
Here's my mother's trick for the potatoes.

234
00:07:50,069 --> 00:07:52,117
Warm the milk and the butter in

235
00:07:52,117 --> 00:07:54,297
a little pan before you add them,

236
00:07:54,297 --> 00:07:56,807
so the potatoes stay fluffy instead of

237
00:07:56,807 --> 00:07:57,732
turning gluey.

238
00:07:57,912 --> 00:08:00,751
And save the drippings from the meatloaf

239
00:08:00,751 --> 00:08:02,383
pan for a little gravy,

240
00:08:02,383 --> 00:08:03,589
if there are any.

241
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:06,137
And here's how to change it up,

242
00:08:06,137 --> 00:08:08,618
once you've got the plain one down.

243
00:08:08,798 --> 00:08:10,975
Put a few strips of bacon over

244
00:08:10,975 --> 00:08:13,080
the top instead of the glaze,

245
00:08:13,080 --> 00:08:15,764
the way Grandma Pearl did on Sundays.

246
00:08:15,944 --> 00:08:18,121
Or hide a row of hard-boiled eggs

247
00:08:18,121 --> 00:08:20,496
down the middle before you shape it,

248
00:08:20,496 --> 00:08:22,739
so every slice has a little circle

249
00:08:22,739 --> 00:08:23,201
of egg.

250
00:08:23,381 --> 00:08:25,549
Or add a cup of shredded cheddar

251
00:08:25,549 --> 00:08:26,295
to the mix.

252
00:08:26,475 --> 00:08:29,844
My grandson calls that one the cheeseburger

253
00:08:29,844 --> 00:08:30,236
loaf.

254
00:08:30,587 --> 00:08:33,051
And the best part of meatloaf is

255
00:08:33,051 --> 00:08:33,744
Thursday.

256
00:08:33,924 --> 00:08:36,226
Cold meatloaf, sliced thin,

257
00:08:36,226 --> 00:08:39,039
on white bread with ketchup and a

258
00:08:39,039 --> 00:08:40,403
leaf of lettuce.

259
00:08:40,583 --> 00:08:43,488
My father took that sandwich to the

260
00:08:43,488 --> 00:08:45,230
plant every Thursday,

261
00:08:45,230 --> 00:08:46,973
wrapped in wax paper,

262
00:08:46,973 --> 00:08:48,965
in his black lunch pail.

263
00:08:49,145 --> 00:08:50,897
Or fry a thick slice in a

264
00:08:50,897 --> 00:08:53,630
skillet with a little butter until it's

265
00:08:53,630 --> 00:08:55,102
crispy on both sides,

266
00:08:55,102 --> 00:08:56,994
and put a fried egg on top.

267
00:08:57,174 --> 00:08:59,519
That's breakfast fit for a king.

268
00:08:59,869 --> 00:09:02,730
It keeps four days in the icebox,

269
00:09:02,730 --> 00:09:03,944
wrapped tight.

270
00:09:04,124 --> 00:09:06,399
And it freezes beautifully.

271
00:09:06,579 --> 00:09:07,836
Slice it first,

272
00:09:07,836 --> 00:09:10,602
put wax paper between the slices,

273
00:09:10,602 --> 00:09:12,698
and freeze them in a bag.

274
00:09:12,878 --> 00:09:15,055
Then you can take out one slice

275
00:09:15,055 --> 00:09:17,092
at a time for a quick supper.

276
00:09:17,272 --> 00:09:20,097
When I lived alone after my husband

277
00:09:20,097 --> 00:09:22,437
passed, that was how I ate my

278
00:09:22,437 --> 00:09:25,585
mother's meatloaf, one slice at a time.

279
00:09:25,935 --> 00:09:28,239
So here it is one more time,

280
00:09:28,239 --> 00:09:28,733
honey.

281
00:09:28,913 --> 00:09:30,515
Eighty-twenty beef.

282
00:09:30,695 --> 00:09:32,413
Soak the crackers in milk.

283
00:09:32,593 --> 00:09:33,742
Fine onion.

284
00:09:33,922 --> 00:09:36,302
Mix gently with your hands.

285
00:09:36,482 --> 00:09:38,141
Shape it on a sheet,

286
00:09:38,141 --> 00:09:39,385
not a loaf pan.

287
00:09:39,565 --> 00:09:42,049
Forty-five minutes, glaze,

288
00:09:42,049 --> 00:09:43,292
fifteen more.

289
00:09:43,472 --> 00:09:45,629
Take it out at one sixty,

290
00:09:45,629 --> 00:09:48,046
and let it rest ten minutes.

291
00:09:48,226 --> 00:09:51,143
That's my mother's Wednesday meatloaf,

292
00:09:51,143 --> 00:09:53,445
and it has never once come out

293
00:09:53,445 --> 00:09:53,752
dry.

294
00:09:54,102 --> 00:09:56,220
Now tell me in the comments,

295
00:09:56,220 --> 00:09:57,051
sweetheart.

296
00:09:57,231 --> 00:10:00,046
What did your family eat every week,

297
00:10:00,046 --> 00:10:01,063
without fail?

298
00:10:01,243 --> 00:10:03,432
Meatloaf, pot roast,

299
00:10:03,432 --> 00:10:05,074
tuna casserole?

300
00:10:05,254 --> 00:10:07,881
Every day I'm bringing you one recipe

301
00:10:07,881 --> 00:10:09,017
from my kitchen,

302
00:10:09,017 --> 00:10:11,503
the way my mother and Grandma Pearl

303
00:10:11,503 --> 00:10:13,846
made it, and I read every comment

304
00:10:13,846 --> 00:10:15,053
for the next one.

305
00:10:15,233 --> 00:10:16,905
Good night, honey.
